    "rem-active-label-complexity-and-noisy-oracles": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-supervised-learning-problem",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **supervised prediction problem** consists of nonempty measurable spaces $(\\mathsf X,\\Sigma_X)$ of instances, $(\\mathsf Y,\\Sigma_Y)$ of labels, and $(\\mathsf A,\\Sigma_A)$ of prediction actions; the product measurable space $(\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y,\\Sigma_X\\otimes\\Sigma_Y)$; a probability measure $P$ on that product; a nonempty class $\\mathcal F$ of measurable predictors $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$; and a loss $\\ell:\\mathsf A\\times\\mathsf Y\\to[0,+\\infty]$ that is measurable with respect to $\\Sigma_A\\otimes\\Sigma_Y$. The law $P$ need not be supported on a deterministic labeling function, and this definition imposes no assumption that sample observations be independent or share a common law.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-population-and-empirical-risk",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a supervised prediction problem with joint law $P$, the **population risk** of any measurable predictor $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$ (in particular, every $f\\in\\mathcal F$) is the extended nonnegative integral\n$$R_P(f):=\\int_{\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y}\\ell(f(x),y)\\,P(d(x,y))\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nFor an ordered sample $S=((x_1,y_1),\\ldots,(x_m,y_m))$ of length $m\\ge1$, the **empirical risk** or **training risk** of any such $f$ is\n$$\\widehat R_S(f):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\ell(f(x_i),y_i)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nNeither definition assumes finite risk. The sum uses extended nonnegative arithmetic; since $m\\ge1$, any summand equal to $+\\infty$ makes the empirical risk $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-population-and-empirical-risk-are-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In a supervised prediction problem, for every measurable predictor $f$, the function $(x,y)\\mapsto\\ell(f(x),y)$ is measurable, so its population risk is a defined extended nonnegative integral. For every sample size $m\\ge1$, the empirical risk $S\\mapsto\\widehat R_S(f)$ is an extended nonnegative measurable function on the finite product sample space.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-realizable-and-agnostic-pac",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal X,\\Sigma_X)$ be a measurable instance space, let the binary label space $\\{0,1\\}$ have its discrete sigma-algebra, and let $\\mathcal H$ be a finite nonempty class of measurable classifiers $h:\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}$. For a probability law $P$ on $\\mathcal X\\times\\{0,1\\}$, write $R_P(h)=P\\{(x,y):h(x)\\ne y\\}$ for the population zero-one risk. A sample of size $m\\ge1$ consists of $m$ independent observations with common law $P$; write $\\widehat R_S(h)=m^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbf 1\\{h(X_i)\\ne Y_i\\}$ for its empirical zero-one risk. The law $P$ is realizable by $\\mathcal H$ if some $h^*\\in\\mathcal H$ satisfies $R_P(h^*)=0$.\n\nPut $\\mathcal Z=\\mathcal X\\times\\{0,1\\}$ with its product sigma-algebra. For fixed $m\\ge1$, a deterministic learner is a measurable map $A:\\mathcal Z^m\\to\\mathcal H$, where the finite set $\\mathcal H$ has its discrete sigma-algebra. For a law $P$ on $\\mathcal Z$, let $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ be an IID sample with common law $P$, and write $\\mathbb P_S$ for probability under its joint law. For $0<\\epsilon,\\delta<1$, the learner is $(\\epsilon,\\delta)$-PAC realizable if, for every law $P$ realizable by $\\mathcal H$, $\\mathbb P_S\\{R_P(A(S))\\le\\epsilon\\}\\ge1-\\delta$. It is agnostic $(\\epsilon,\\delta)$-PAC if, for every law $P$ on $\\mathcal Z$, $\\mathbb P_S\\{R_P(A(S))-\\inf_{h\\in\\mathcal H}R_P(h)\\le\\epsilon\\}\\ge1-\\delta$. A randomized learner uses a measurable probability space $(\\mathcal U,\\Sigma_U,Q)$ and a measurable output map $A:\\mathcal Z^m\\times\\mathcal U\\to\\mathcal H$, with a seed $U\\sim Q$ independent of $S$; in both criteria replace $A(S)$ by $A(S,U)$ and $\\mathbb P_S$ by the joint probability $\\mathbb P_{S,U}$. All risks and the infimum are finite because the zero-one loss is bounded by one. These definitions impose no computational-efficiency claim.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-pool-based-realizable-active-learning",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal X,\\Sigma)$ be a nonempty measurable space, let $D_X$ be a probability measure on it, and let $\\mathcal H$ be a finite nonempty set of measurable classifiers $h:\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}$. A finite pool-based realizable active-learning instance consists of an ordered pool $U=(u_1,\\ldots,u_m)\\in\\mathcal X^m$ for some $m\\ge0$, an unknown target $h^*\\in\\mathcal H$, and a deterministic exact label oracle $\\mathcal O:\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}$ satisfying $\\mathcal O(x)=h^*(x)$ at every queried point. A fresh feature $X\\sim D_X$ has target label $h^*(X)$; the pool itself need not be sampled from $D_X$. For a set $Q\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ of queried indices with observed labels $(y_i)_{i\\in Q}$, the version space is $V(Q,y)=\\{h\\in\\mathcal H:h(u_i)=y_i\\text{ for every }i\\in Q\\}$. For any $V\\subseteq\\mathcal H$, its disagreement region is $\\operatorname{DIS}(V)=\\{x\\in\\mathcal X:\\exists h,g\\in V,\\ h(x)\\ne g(x)\\}$. Since $V$ is finite and every classifier is measurable, $\\operatorname{DIS}(V)$ is measurable. The pointwise exact-oracle assumption is stronger than standard distributional PAC realizability: zero population risk only requires agreement almost surely under the joint law and does not prescribe oracle values at every pool point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "alg-finite-pool-version-space-query",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Input a finite pool-based realizable active-learning instance with ordered pool $U=(u_1,\\ldots,u_m)$ and a nonnegative integer query budget $b$. Initialize the current version space $V=\\mathcal H$, query count $q=0$, pool index $i=1$, an empty query log, and an empty status record for each pool index. While $i\\le m$, first check whether every $h\\in V$ gives the same label at $u_i$. If so, record that unanimous label as a prediction and set $i\\leftarrow i+1$, even when $q=b$. Otherwise, if $q=b$, mark indices $i,i+1,\\ldots,m$ as abstentions and return the current $V$, records, and query log. If instead $q<b$, query $y=\\mathcal O(u_i)$, append $(i,y)$ to the query log, record the queried label, replace $V$ by $\\{h\\in V:h(u_i)=y\\}$, set $q\\leftarrow q+1$ and $i\\leftarrow i+1$, and, if now $q=b$, mark all remaining indices $i,\\ldots,m$ as abstentions and return. If the scan reaches $i=m+1$, return the current $V$, records, and query log. The query budget limits oracle calls; the procedure scans each pool index at most once and reports unresolved suffix entries as abstentions when it stops at the budget.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-active-version-space-invariant-and-query-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal H$ be a finite nonempty class of binary classifiers, let $U=(u_1,\\ldots,u_m)$ be a finite ordered pool with $m\\ge0$, let $h^*\\in\\mathcal H$, let the deterministic oracle return $h^*(u_i)$ at every query, and run the finite-pool version-space query procedure with any integer budget $b\\ge0$. At every stage the current version space contains $h^*$ and is nonempty; each pool label recorded as a unanimous prediction equals $h^*$ at that point; and the procedure terminates after at most $m$ pool iterations. If it makes $q$ queries, then $q\\le\\min\\{m,|\\mathcal H|-1\\}$. Moreover, on any execution for which each oracle-selected branch has size at most $\\rho$ times its pre-query version space, for a fixed $0<\\rho<1$, one has $q\\le\\log |\\mathcal H|/\\log(1/\\rho)$, where $\\log$ is the natural logarithm.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-disagreement-abstention-correctness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix a finite pool-based realizable active-learning instance, run the ascending-scan procedure for any finite number of steps, and let $V$ be the resulting current version space. Let $X\\sim D_X$ be a fresh feature independent of the query history. Define the selective prediction rule $g_V(x)$ to be the common label $c\\in\\{0,1\\}$ when every $h\\in V$ has $h(x)=c$, and to be the abstention symbol $\\bot$ otherwise. Then $\\operatorname{DIS}(V)$ is measurable,\n$$D_X(\\{x:g_V(x)=\\bot\\})=D_X(\\operatorname{DIS}(V)),$$\nand\n$$D_X(\\{x:g_V(x)\\in\\{0,1\\},\\ g_V(x)\\ne h^*(x)\\})=0.$$\nThese probabilities are for a fixed query history; the formula gives the abstention probability but no automatic upper bound on it from $|V|$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Let $\\mathcal X=\\{a,b\\}$ with its full sigma-algebra and label set $\\{0,1\\}$ with its discrete sigma-algebra. Take the joint law $P=\\delta_{(a,0)}$, use zero-one loss $\\ell(\\hat y,y)=\\mathbf 1\\{\\hat y\\ne y\\}$, and set $D_X=P_X=\\delta_a$, where $P_X$ is the input marginal of $P$. Define $\\mathcal H=\\{h_0,h_1\\}$ by $h_0(a)=h_0(b)=0$ and $h_1(a)=0$, $h_1(b)=1$. For pool $U=(b)$, choose target $h^*=h_1$ and exact oracle $\\mathcal O=h_1$. The zero-one error events are measurable by [F1] and [F3], and [F2] defines their risks as their $P$-probabilities. Both risks are zero, so [F4] declares the law realizable by either classifier. Yet $h_0$ and $h^*$ give different labels at the pool point $b$, whose $D_X$-probability is zero. Thus distributional realizability does not prescribe the label at every possible pool point; [F5]'s pointwise oracle condition is a genuinely stronger interface.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Let $\\mathcal X=\\{a\\}$, $\\mathcal H=\\{h_0,h_1\\}$ with $h_0(a)=0$ and $h_1(a)=1$, target $h^*=h_0$, and budget $b\\ge1$. If the filtering update is fed the noisy response $1$ at the initial disagreement $a$, [F6] replaces $V=\\{h_0,h_1\\}$ by $\\{h_1\\}$, deleting the true target. Hence the invariant proof cannot be extended to arbitrary noisy responses.",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1-3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "For any $m\\ge2$, take $\\mathcal X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$, target $h_*(x_j)=1$, alternatives $h_i(x_j)=0$ exactly when $j=i$, pool $(x_1,\\ldots,x_m)$, and budget $b\\ge m$. Before query $i$, the version space is $\\{h_*,h_i,\\ldots,h_m\\}$: this is true initially and, at $x_i$, the exact positive label removes only $h_i$. Thus all $m$ indices are queried and $q=m=|\\mathcal H|-1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Set $m=2^k-1$ for integers $k\\ge2$. Then $|\\mathcal H|=2^k$ and $q=2^k-1$. By repeated application of [F10]'s product law, $\\log|\\mathcal H|=k\\log2$, and strict increase with $2>1$ gives $\\log2>\\log1=0$. The ratio $q/(k\\log2)$ is at least $2^{k-1}/(k\\log2)$; the latter is unbounded because its consecutive-term ratio is $2k/(k+1)\\ge4/3$. Therefore no uniform constant multiple of $\\log|\\mathcal H|$ bounds the query count, while [F7]'s logarithmic guarantee remains valid when its branch-shrink premise holds.",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Let $\\mathcal X=\\{a\\}$, $\\mathcal H=\\{h_0,h_1\\}$ with $h_0(a)=0$, $h_1(a)=1$, target $h^*=h_0$, current version space $V=\\mathcal H$, and $D_X(\\{a\\})=1$. The initial version space is consistent with the exact-target model. Then $\\operatorname{DIS}(V)=\\mathcal X$, so [F8] gives abstention probability $D_X(\\operatorname{DIS}(V))=1$ although $|V|=2$. This verifies that finiteness alone does not force the disagreement mass to be small. All witnesses are explicit finite constructions, as stated in [A1].",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F5",
            "F8",
            "construct"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The star-class witness assumes $m\\ge2$.",
